00:03:34.840 --> 00:03:40.200
This trade demonstrates how to size a position around a single high-conviction data release while

00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:46.140
using a defined stop to protect capital. Listeners learned to distinguish between a news-driven spike and

00:03:46.140 --> 00:03:52.700
a sustained move by requiring volume confirmation on the entry day. Last week's simulated hold was on

00:03:52.700 --> 00:04:01.280
NVO with entry at $45.39 on Monday open. The exit came at $43.75 on Friday close.

00:04:01.280 --> 00:04:11.720
The result was a loss of 3.61% or $36.13 on a $1,000 position. The trade lost 3.61%

00:04:11.720 --> 00:04:18.060
while the NASDAQ rose during the same window, producing negative alpha. The launch catalyst produced

00:04:18.060 --> 00:04:23.460
no sustained follow-through once broader market volatility from geopolitical headlines took over.

00:04:24.080 --> 00:04:29.200
This is the exact scenario where our earlier rule about volume confirmation would have helped.

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:35.840
The lesson learned is to require both a fundamental catalyst and a volume spike above the 20-day

